1rlz|v|To sink, in the middle, by its weight or under applied pressure, below a horizontal line or plane; as, a line or cable supported by its ends sags, though tightly drawn; the floor of a room sags; hence, to lean, give way, or settle from a vertical position; as, a building may sag one way or another; a door sags on its hinges. ; Fig.: To lose firmness or elasticity; to sink; to droop; to flag; to bend; to yield, as the mind or spirits, under the pressure of care, trouble, doubt, or the like; to be unsettled or unbalanced. ; To loiter in walking; to idle along; to drag or droop heavily. ; To cause to bend or give way; to load.	s:1zvb,47,1~1zvb,47,1	D:22p7,13,4~22p7,13,4
